out.push(note('Teacher\'s note. Four faults to watch, and the remedy for each. First, preparing for the wrong hour. Learners arrive at a meeting with eleven pages for a discussion or one page for a decision, and in every case they had the invitation days in advance and did not ask. Drill the three questions as a fixed set and insist on the third in the form given — what would a good hour look like at ten? — because "what is this about?" is answerable from the paper and buys nothing. Watch also for the answer that comes back as a topic rather than a state of affairs: the repair is the follow-up, what would be TRUE at ten that is not true now. Second, the motive written as an interest. It is the most reliable fault in the unit and it attaches itself to whoever the learner likes least in the room. The remedy is arithmetical rather than moral: ask for the figure. An interest with a quantity in it can be checked against what the person has said and done, and in the series\' own trial two learners in five found that the motive they had written was the exact reverse of the interest — most often about a colleague who hoards information and has in fact asked twice to be relieved of it. Third, the four-second failure at 10.4. Learners have the fact, have it in time, and lose the moment composing a sentence. That is why the opening is taught as a fixed formula and not as a principle: four words can be deployed in half a second, where a principle has to be turned into words while somebody else is still speaking. Drill it until it is automatic, and drill the stop as well, which is harder — correcting a basis and taking a position are two interventions, and joined together they force the other person to defend his item in order to keep it. Fourth, and this is the one that outlives the unit: correcting a minute on substance. The slide happens at a conjunction — "and in any case", "anyway", "which means that" — and it is invisible from the inside, so make a partner catch the exact word. The argument for two envelopes is not etiquette and should not be taught as etiquette: a chairman who sees an argument inside a correction must read the whole note as an attempt to improve the record, and the safe answer to that is no to all of it, including the accuracy point he would have granted in four seconds. One practical note on 10.7. The remedy in that cycle comes from the minute-taker and not from the learner, and that is deliberate. Mr Tarabishi has written "noted" for nineteen years because nobody has ever read a minute against a note and told him; and when he is read to properly he applies 9.4 better than Nour does, refusing to write a date that nobody in the room chose. Make the class notice that. The habit worth leaving them with is the one he asks for: take your own note, with the times on it, and get it to whoever is writing the record before it is circulated — because the corrected minute is the record and the first circulation is what people remember.'));
